[FINAL-TEXT] The evidence is now conclusive. The iptables counter tells the full story after the test:

- **Rule 1 ACCEPT (ICMP)**: 4 packets matched — exactly the 4 ICMP echo requests from the `ping pc1→pc3` test. Ping to `10.3.0.100` works.
- **Rule 2 DROP (all)**: 3 packets matched — exactly the TCP SYNs from `nc -z -w3 10.3.0.100 80` (initial + 2 retransmits). TCP to `10.3.0.100` is silently dropped.
- **nc to `10.5.0.4`**: got `exit=1` because **port 80 simply isn't listening on srv1** — zero packets matched either iptables rule, confirming `10.5.0.4` is *not* affected by the firewall.

The fault is fully localised:

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1
diagnosis_text:
An iptables FORWARD chain ACL was injected on dswa1, the Office-A
distribution switch, that silently drops all non-ICMP traffic from the
Office-A user VLAN (10.1.0.0/24) destined for the Office-B VLAN-10 client
subnet (10.3.0.0/24). The specific rules are, in order:

  ACCEPT  proto 1 (ICMP)  -i br0.10  -s 10.1.0.0/24  -d 10.3.0.0/24
  DROP    all traffic      -i br0.10  -s 10.1.0.0/24  -d 10.3.0.0/24

Because ICMP is explicitly permitted before the catch-all DROP, ping from
PC1 (10.1.0.100) to hosts in 10.3.0.0/24 (e.g. PC3 at 10.3.0.100) succeeds,
masking the fault from a simple connectivity check. All TCP and UDP sessions
from Office-A users to that subnet are silently discarded at dswa1's kernel
netfilter layer before FRR ever touches them. The Office-B server subnet
(10.5.0.0/24) is entirely unaffected — the injected rules do not match that
destination, so connectivity from PC1 to srv1 (10.5.0.4) is fully intact
(confirmed 5/5 ping, zero iptables hits for 10.5.0.0/24 traffic). The
helpdesk ticket's assertion that "10.5.0.4 is unreachable" is therefore a
mis-description of the symptom; the real user-visible breakage is TCP/UDP
sessions from Office A to Office-B VLAN-10 clients. The root cause is an
iptables rule added to dswa1's FORWARD chain that incorrectly targets
10.3.0.0/24 instead of (or in addition to) the intended destination. The
corrective fix is to delete both rules from dswa1:
  iptables -D FORWARD -p icmp -i br0.10 -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -j ACCEPT
  iptables -D FORWARD      -i br0.10 -s 10.1.0.0/24 -d 10.3.0.0/24 -j DROP
=== END ===
```